\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Conceptual setup of the SMB-inference pipeline, illustrated for Great Aletsch Glacier. The panels show the observed surface elevation at the start (2009) and end (2017) of the inversion window. Prior to the inversion, data assimilation recovers the ice thickness---and hence the bedrock---at the 2009 epoch, constrained by the observed surface-velocity field \citep  {millan2022} (Sect.\nobreakspace  {}\hbox {}). The transient glacier-evolution model (GEM) integrates the 2009 geometry forward to 2017 under a candidate elevation-dependent surface mass balance, and the inversion seeks the mass balance for which the modelled 2017 geometry reproduces the observed one (Sect.\nobreakspace  {}\hbox {}). The dashed line marks the observed 2017 terminus, highlighting the retreat of the tongue over the eight-year window. Colour denotes surface elevation.\relax }}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
